WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.040
Imagine you're at a silent charity auction.

00:03.220 --> 00:04.180
Okay, I'm picturing it.

00:04.260 --> 00:04.460
Right.

00:04.800 --> 00:09.660
So a guy walks into the room, he bids $1,000 on this beautiful painting, and he loses.

00:10.000 --> 00:11.620
He loses to someone who bid higher.

00:12.060 --> 00:13.140
Yeah, happens all the time.

00:13.240 --> 00:13.600
Exactly.

00:13.920 --> 00:16.600
He doesn't take the painting home, he gets absolutely nothing.

00:17.100 --> 00:22.860
But simply because he was in the room holding up a paddle, the actual winner is forced to

00:22.860 --> 00:25.620
pay thousands of dollars more than they would have otherwise.

00:25.920 --> 00:26.360
Oh, wow.

00:26.360 --> 00:31.600
Yeah, and what if I told you that exact scam, like that exact mathematical squeeze, is

00:31.600 --> 00:36.720
happening in your social media feed right now, millions of times a second, and it is dictating

00:36.720 --> 00:38.140
literally everything you see.

00:38.300 --> 00:41.040
I mean, it sounds like a totally broken market when you frame it that way.

00:41.420 --> 00:43.980
You'd assume the auctioneer is running a racket.

00:44.280 --> 00:44.340
Right.

00:44.840 --> 00:48.580
But in the digital space, that is simply the baseline architecture.

00:49.200 --> 00:53.980
It's the accepted invisible law of gravity that governs how human attention is bought

00:53.980 --> 00:54.460
and sold.

00:54.680 --> 00:54.980
Okay.

00:54.980 --> 01:00.160
Let's unpack this, because that invisible architecture is exactly what we're going to

01:00.160 --> 01:01.580
explore today in this deep dive.

01:02.240 --> 01:03.700
It's a massive topic.

01:03.980 --> 01:04.760
It really is.

01:05.100 --> 01:10.400
We are looking at this fascinating and frankly kind of terrifying analytical essay published

01:10.400 --> 01:12.600
back in November 2026.

01:12.800 --> 01:15.640
It's by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

01:15.980 --> 01:16.240
Right.

01:16.800 --> 01:20.300
Platform Control and the Political Economy of Audience Access.

01:20.480 --> 01:21.060
That's the one.

01:21.060 --> 01:25.420
And our mission for you today is to tear this dense structural analysis apart.

01:25.660 --> 01:30.740
We want to look past the user interface, past, you know, the notification bells and the little

01:30.740 --> 01:31.500
red badges.

01:31.840 --> 01:33.700
Get into the actual plumbing of it all.

01:33.780 --> 01:34.160
Exactly.

01:34.460 --> 01:34.600
Yeah.

01:35.000 --> 01:40.800
The cold hard math of platform algorithms, the digital auctions, and the actual quantifiable

01:40.800 --> 01:43.100
toll that's being extracted from your attention.

01:43.100 --> 01:47.500
And I think that toll is perhaps the most vital takeaway from this research.

01:48.320 --> 01:51.540
Because the essay, it doesn't deal in moral panic.

01:51.760 --> 01:52.380
No, not at all.

01:52.480 --> 01:57.620
It doesn't judge whether a specific app is ruining society or if like a certain type of

01:57.620 --> 01:58.700
video is rotting our brains.

01:58.860 --> 02:02.580
It simply looks at the incentives, the raw economics.

02:02.580 --> 02:07.000
The structural realities of how information actually gets from a producer to you, the

02:07.000 --> 02:07.380
consumer.

02:07.840 --> 02:08.180
Exactly.

02:08.980 --> 02:11.700
So the paper starts at the very foundation of social media.

02:11.960 --> 02:17.160
It starts with this huge fundamental illusion that we all basically buy into regarding our

02:17.160 --> 02:18.260
digital relationships.

02:18.560 --> 02:20.120
The illusion of ownership.

02:20.740 --> 02:21.120
Yes.

02:21.400 --> 02:22.860
Let's think about a creator for a second.

02:23.120 --> 02:24.620
Say a local baker.

02:25.100 --> 02:30.540
They spend five years posting tutorials, sharing recipes, answering comments at two in the morning.

02:30.860 --> 02:32.040
Putting in serious sweat equity.

02:32.040 --> 02:32.360
Right.

02:32.680 --> 02:35.580
And slowly, they build an audience of 50,000 people.

02:36.120 --> 02:40.380
Now, those 50,000 people made a deliberate conscious choice to click follow.

02:40.840 --> 02:41.500
They opted in.

02:41.720 --> 02:41.920
Yeah.

02:42.180 --> 02:42.740
They opted in.

02:43.040 --> 02:47.200
It feels like the baker built a house, invited all their friends over, and now they're all

02:47.200 --> 02:48.780
just hanging out in the living room together.

02:49.260 --> 02:50.080
It feels that way.

02:50.160 --> 02:50.360
Mm-hmm.

02:50.540 --> 02:53.720
And the baker certainly put in the labor and the capital to build that house.

02:53.800 --> 02:53.920
Right.

02:54.480 --> 02:59.720
But the asymmetry here, the catch, is that the relationship is completely dependent on a delivery

02:59.720 --> 03:02.120
system that the baker does not control.

03:02.300 --> 03:03.120
Like, at all.

03:03.360 --> 03:03.640
At all.

03:03.720 --> 03:06.220
The platform owns the roads leading to the house.

03:06.460 --> 03:07.300
They own the front door.

03:07.620 --> 03:08.820
They literally own the locks.

03:08.900 --> 03:09.040
Yeah.

03:09.120 --> 03:13.060
So, the baker spends all this time building the community, but the landlord is standing

03:13.060 --> 03:17.260
at the front door deciding which of those 50,000 friends actually get to step inside.

03:17.400 --> 03:17.800
Exactly.

03:17.800 --> 03:22.760
And simultaneously, that same landlord is, like, charging random strangers a cover charge

03:22.760 --> 03:24.920
to just parachute into the baker's living room.

03:25.080 --> 03:26.920
That captures the dynamic perfectly.

03:27.340 --> 03:31.900
I mean, the source material explains that this platform control essentially has two faces.

03:32.120 --> 03:32.340
Okay.

03:32.380 --> 03:32.720
What are they?

03:33.260 --> 03:35.740
The first face is control over access.

03:36.480 --> 03:41.840
Can the baker actually reach the followers who explicitly opted in to hear from them?

03:41.860 --> 03:42.060
Right.

03:42.120 --> 03:42.580
The delivery.

03:42.920 --> 03:43.100
Yeah.

03:43.540 --> 03:45.900
And the second face is control over avoidance.

03:45.900 --> 03:51.200
If you, as a user, tell the platform you never want to see content from a specific brand

03:51.200 --> 03:54.920
ever again, does the platform actually honor that refusal?

03:55.020 --> 03:55.180
Okay.

03:55.240 --> 03:56.840
Do they actually keep the door closed?

03:57.140 --> 03:57.560
Exactly.

03:58.200 --> 04:03.820
Both of these come down to how far a recorded preference, like a follow or a hide, is actually

04:03.820 --> 04:06.000
permitted to govern future delivery.

04:06.440 --> 04:06.780
But wait.

04:06.860 --> 04:11.620
I mean, when I click follow, I am actively telling the algorithm what I want, right?

04:11.660 --> 04:12.600
That's a direct connection.

04:13.100 --> 04:15.020
I'm literally curating my own feed.

04:15.020 --> 04:17.160
You feel like you're curating it.

04:17.300 --> 04:17.440
Yeah.

04:17.620 --> 04:21.880
But the reality is you're merely submitting a suggestion to a black box.

04:22.280 --> 04:22.900
A suggestion.

04:23.200 --> 04:23.460
Yeah.

04:23.940 --> 04:24.200
Yeah.

04:24.460 --> 04:28.300
Every time you click follow, you're just handing the platform a data point.

04:28.720 --> 04:34.060
The platform then takes your suggestion, puts it on a scale, and weighs it against its

04:34.060 --> 04:34.940
own revenue models.

04:35.120 --> 04:36.280
It's engagement predictions.

04:36.640 --> 04:37.100
Exactly.

04:37.260 --> 04:38.720
And it's available ad inventory.

04:38.720 --> 04:45.260
Your deliberate choice is just one tiny, highly negotiable input in this massive optimization

04:45.260 --> 04:45.760
engine.

04:45.760 --> 04:49.920
And that engine is designed to maximize their yield, not my satisfaction.

04:50.280 --> 04:50.680
100%.

04:50.680 --> 04:53.180
Which is wild to think about.

04:53.560 --> 04:57.800
My act of choice is just a suggestion that can be vetoed in a millisecond.

04:57.800 --> 05:01.700
But, you know, it didn't always feel this restrictive, did it?

05:01.820 --> 05:02.760
No, it didn't.

05:02.840 --> 05:07.660
Like, I remember a time when if you followed someone, you just saw their stuff in chronological

05:07.660 --> 05:08.000
order.

05:08.780 --> 05:12.180
How did we get from that to this heavily gated reality?

05:12.660 --> 05:16.300
Well, the author provides a really illuminating chronological breakdown of this.

05:16.640 --> 05:20.440
It traces how organic reach has been systematically throttled over the years.

05:20.860 --> 05:23.440
Based on the platform's own public announcements, right?

05:23.680 --> 05:24.000
Yes.

05:24.120 --> 05:24.800
Their own words.

05:24.800 --> 05:30.900
If we trace the timeline back to 2014, platforms were already publicly warning businesses that

05:30.900 --> 05:33.420
organic reach for their pages was going to decline.

05:33.880 --> 05:34.220
Oh, man.

05:34.260 --> 05:35.180
I remember that era.

05:35.440 --> 05:38.500
Every brand and publisher was panicking.

05:38.760 --> 05:39.260
Total panic.

05:39.520 --> 05:44.800
Because they had spent millions of dollars running campaigns to get these likes under the promise

05:44.800 --> 05:47.920
that a like meant free access to that customer forever.

05:48.180 --> 05:48.360
Right.

05:48.740 --> 05:50.080
And then the rug got pulled.

05:50.240 --> 05:51.080
Completely pulled.

05:51.080 --> 05:56.180
The platform's stated justification back in 2014 was simply volume.

05:56.800 --> 06:01.280
They argued that because more users and businesses were joining, there just wasn't enough physical

06:01.280 --> 06:03.180
space in the feed to show everything.

06:03.700 --> 06:06.400
It was framed as just a natural consequence of growth.

06:06.560 --> 06:06.900
Exactly.

06:07.180 --> 06:13.720
But crucially, in the exact same breath, they pitched paid media advertising as the predictable,

06:13.940 --> 06:14.800
reliable alternative.

06:15.700 --> 06:16.640
Oh, of course they did.

06:16.640 --> 06:21.480
They effectively told businesses, look, organic reach is a gamble now, but if you want guaranteed

06:21.480 --> 06:25.360
distribution to the audience you already built, you can just pay us.

06:25.720 --> 06:29.440
The landlord setting up a VIP line with a cover charge to get into your own house.

06:29.700 --> 06:30.040
That's it.

06:30.660 --> 06:35.640
And then we move forward to 2018, which marked a massive structural shift.

06:35.640 --> 06:41.200
A major ranking update was announced that deprioritized content from publishers and businesses.

06:42.200 --> 06:42.940
In favor of what?

06:43.360 --> 06:48.740
In favor of posts expected to prompt, quote, conversations and meaningful interactions.

06:49.180 --> 06:50.300
Ah, right.

06:50.760 --> 06:55.540
The announcement explicitly warned page owners that they would likely see significant decreases

06:55.540 --> 06:56.940
in reach and watch time.

06:56.940 --> 07:02.620
So suddenly, the suggestion of a follow was completely overridden by a machine learning

07:02.620 --> 07:02.960
model.

07:03.320 --> 07:07.140
A model just guessing whether or not the post would make people argue in the comments.

07:07.480 --> 07:08.720
Yeah, it was the practical outcome, yeah.

07:09.340 --> 07:14.260
A follow became completely insufficient for delivery unless the algorithm predicted interaction.

07:14.580 --> 07:15.140
That's crazy.

07:15.840 --> 07:21.060
Now, the platform did introduce a see-first feature around this time, allowing a user to

07:21.060 --> 07:24.200
manually tell the system to prioritize a specific page.

07:24.560 --> 07:26.420
Okay, so they gave a little control back.

07:26.420 --> 07:32.460
A little, but this is a vital precedent because it proved the platform absolutely had the technical

07:32.460 --> 07:38.740
capability to build a mechanism where a user's explicit request overrides the algorithmic

07:38.740 --> 07:39.040
sorting.

07:39.760 --> 07:41.680
They could build a feed that respects your choices.

07:42.180 --> 07:42.520
Exactly.

07:43.120 --> 07:46.420
But they chose to make it in an obscure setting rather than the default.

07:47.140 --> 07:49.160
Because the default is where the money is.

07:49.360 --> 07:49.660
Always.

07:50.780 --> 07:56.120
And by 2022, I mean, the whole concept of a follow-based feed was basically blown to pieces

07:56.120 --> 07:56.520
anyway.

07:57.400 --> 07:59.040
The architecture fractured completely.

08:00.020 --> 08:05.460
The platform introduced a separate feeds tab where you could theoretically see chronological

08:05.460 --> 08:06.840
posts from people you follow.

08:07.500 --> 08:08.720
But no one used it.

08:09.000 --> 08:13.360
Because the default tab, the one that opens automatically when you launch the app, literally

08:13.360 --> 08:21.180
named the Home tab, was explicitly redesigned as a discovery engine, driven entirely by algorithmic

08:21.180 --> 08:21.700
recommendation.

08:22.200 --> 08:27.080
Meaning the default experience we all have every day is less about the people we actively

08:27.080 --> 08:31.680
chose to connect with and more about what a behavioral prediction model thinks will keep

08:31.680 --> 08:33.700
our thumbs moving for another three seconds.

08:33.700 --> 08:34.220
Right.

08:34.680 --> 08:40.260
And of course, the ads, the paid placements, are injected directly into that endless scroll

08:40.260 --> 08:41.000
of recommendations.

08:41.360 --> 08:41.480
Man.

08:42.360 --> 08:42.620
Okay.

08:42.700 --> 08:47.780
So everything we just discussed is about how ranking and algorithms throttle a follow-request.

08:48.160 --> 08:53.900
But the source text brings up a much more extreme, absolute form of platform control,

08:54.000 --> 08:54.180
right?

08:54.440 --> 08:54.920
Yes, it does.

08:55.020 --> 08:57.140
Where the ranking algorithm doesn't even factor in.

08:57.140 --> 09:02.660
And that is the concept of eligibility, which they illustrate with the Canadian news case

09:02.660 --> 09:03.380
from 2023.

09:03.920 --> 09:06.220
This is a perfect example of eligibility.

09:06.660 --> 09:06.780
Right.

09:07.580 --> 09:12.340
And before we dive into this, we really need to pause and be incredibly clear with you,

09:12.400 --> 09:12.740
the listener.

09:12.980 --> 09:14.000
Yes, absolutely.

09:14.340 --> 09:19.160
The author of this essay and both of us as your hosts today are taking absolutely no political

09:19.160 --> 09:24.000
sides regarding the Canadian legislation, the Online News Act, or the platform's response

09:24.000 --> 09:24.360
to it.

09:24.460 --> 09:24.960
None at all.

09:25.140 --> 09:26.140
We're not judging the government.

09:26.140 --> 09:27.860
We are not judging the tech companies.

09:28.560 --> 09:34.580
We are strictly analyzing the structural, architectural reality of the event as it's documented in

09:34.580 --> 09:35.240
the source material.

09:35.540 --> 09:39.720
We are completely impartial observers just looking at the mechanics of the machine here.

09:40.240 --> 09:40.980
Very important disclaimer.

09:41.380 --> 09:45.760
So with that understood, let's look at what actually happened structurally.

09:46.300 --> 09:51.440
In August 2023, the platform announced it was ending news availability for all users in

09:51.440 --> 09:53.720
Canada to comply with the federal legislation.

09:53.880 --> 09:54.080
Right.

09:54.080 --> 09:59.800
And as of November 2026, the paper notes this restriction was still largely in effect, pending

09:59.800 --> 10:00.800
various trade talks.

10:01.800 --> 10:06.840
Structurally, this represents the ultimate, most severe form of platform control.

10:07.720 --> 10:09.100
Control over eligibility.

10:09.100 --> 10:11.600
Let me make sure I'm wrapping my head around this.

10:11.820 --> 10:13.280
Let's say I live in Toronto.

10:13.280 --> 10:17.640
For 10 years, I have followed my local city newspapers page.

10:17.860 --> 10:22.140
I want to know about road closures, local elections, community events.

10:22.260 --> 10:23.240
Standard local news.

10:23.240 --> 10:26.960
I have explicitly requested that direct connection.

10:26.960 --> 10:30.460
But the platform can just pull the plug at the front door?

10:30.960 --> 10:35.800
The platform can entirely withdraw the eligibility of the source to be distributed at all.

10:35.920 --> 10:36.260
Wow.

10:36.880 --> 10:41.320
Before the ranking algorithm even boots up to decide if the news article is engaging, before

10:41.320 --> 10:45.240
it checks if it will spark a conversation, the door is locked.

10:45.240 --> 10:46.280
Just locked tight.

10:46.660 --> 10:50.980
The platform identified news outlets based on the definitions in the legislation and simply

10:50.980 --> 10:53.320
turned off their ability to reach Canadian users.

10:53.940 --> 10:59.160
Your 10-year history of following them, your explicit recorded preference, meant absolutely

10:59.160 --> 11:02.240
nothing in the face of the platform's eligibility switch.

11:02.240 --> 11:03.700
That is...

11:03.700 --> 11:06.260
Let's talk about what that actually means for the producers.

11:06.800 --> 11:12.080
Because the paper makes a really profound point about the fundamental economics of reporting.

11:12.400 --> 11:14.620
It's a brutal economic reality.

11:14.820 --> 11:18.520
Like, let's imagine a local reporter covering a municipal zoning board.

11:18.580 --> 11:19.880
That is not glamorous work.

11:20.280 --> 11:20.520
No.

11:21.180 --> 11:23.880
It is incredibly expensive, time-consuming work.

11:24.000 --> 11:28.980
I mean, for that news to even exist in the world, a human being has to physically drive

11:28.980 --> 11:30.300
to the municipal building.

11:30.300 --> 11:32.180
Sit in a fluorescent lit room?

11:32.400 --> 11:34.720
For four hours listening to zoning debates?

11:35.180 --> 11:35.540
Exactly.

11:36.180 --> 11:41.480
Request public budget documents, read through hundreds of pages of legal jargon, and then

11:41.480 --> 11:43.600
interview local officials just to make sense of it all.

11:44.040 --> 11:44.960
The real work.

11:45.460 --> 11:47.140
The work that actually informs a community.

11:47.760 --> 11:50.160
And that work requires massive upfront capital.

11:50.820 --> 11:52.020
That reporter needs a salary.

11:52.480 --> 11:53.640
They need health insurance.

11:53.900 --> 11:56.280
They need housing and food during the weeks or months.

11:56.280 --> 12:01.380
They're building local sources and investigating a story that, frankly, might end up being a dead

12:01.380 --> 12:01.600
end.

12:01.600 --> 12:02.000
Right.

12:02.100 --> 12:06.500
The newsroom pays for all of that capacity, all of that infrastructure, before a single

12:06.500 --> 12:07.460
word is published.

12:08.060 --> 12:13.200
They take all the financial risk upfront before they have any idea how widely the final story

12:13.200 --> 12:16.900
will travel or how much advertising revenue it might generate.

12:16.900 --> 12:31.540
But the delivery of that finished, incredibly expensive product is controlled entirely by a third party, a platform that contributed $0 to the reporter's salary, paid nothing for the public records requests, took none of the upfront risk.

12:31.540 --> 12:39.840
The platform bears none of the production costs, yet it single-handedly shapes the conditions under which the producer can actually recover their investment.

12:40.000 --> 12:40.680
It's staggering.

12:40.680 --> 12:44.780
The Canadian case is the absolute limiting instance of this power dynamic.

12:44.780 --> 12:48.280
A user recorded a choice to hear from an outlet.

12:48.280 --> 12:56.580
The outlet spent thousands of dollars producing the journalism, and the platform simply changed the rules of who was eligible to reach that reader.

12:56.580 --> 12:57.720
Just flipped a switch.

12:57.720 --> 13:00.240
It proves that a follow does not secure delivery.

13:00.580 --> 13:01.760
It does not secure reach.

13:02.280 --> 13:06.120
It doesn't even secure the basic eligibility to participate in the feed.

13:06.120 --> 13:07.900
That is just heavy.

13:08.460 --> 13:18.100
So if you're a business, a creator, or a publisher, organic access is heavily restricted, algorithmically gated, and sometimes entirely revoked without warning.

13:18.340 --> 13:20.280
Which leaves you with exactly one option.

13:20.540 --> 13:23.740
If you want to reliably reach the audience you spent years building.

13:24.040 --> 13:25.480
You have to use the paid alternative.

13:26.100 --> 13:28.840
You have to buy your way into the living room of the house you built.

13:29.200 --> 13:33.280
Which brings us to the core analytical model of the essay, the invisible auction.

13:33.640 --> 13:34.700
Oh, this is the good stuff.

13:34.700 --> 13:41.460
Because if organic reach is choked off, how exactly is your attention priced and sold in the paid ecosystem?

13:42.040 --> 13:43.860
Here's where it gets really interesting.

13:44.100 --> 13:47.680
Because the paper breaks this down using a simple auction model for fixed capacity.

13:48.000 --> 13:52.220
So let's pull back the curtain and look at the math behind every single ad you scroll past.

13:52.380 --> 13:52.640
Right.

13:53.040 --> 13:58.620
The author strips the auction down to its most basic economic components to reveal the underlying incentives.

13:58.880 --> 13:59.760
Okay, walk me through it.

13:59.760 --> 14:04.820
Imagine there are a fixed number of ad placements available in your feed on any given day.

14:05.180 --> 14:07.900
Let's call that finite number of slots K.

14:08.500 --> 14:08.820
Okay.

14:09.200 --> 14:09.980
K slots.

14:10.300 --> 14:16.720
Now imagine there are a massive number of advertisers, far more than K, who desperately want those spots.

14:16.720 --> 14:20.260
So we have scarcity, high demand, limited supply.

14:20.920 --> 14:24.940
The text starts by looking at a scenario where everyone values the ad spot equally, right?

14:25.320 --> 14:28.240
Let's assume every single bidder in the auction has done their math.

14:28.580 --> 14:33.900
And they've determined that getting an ad in your feed is worth exactly $10 to their business.

14:34.420 --> 14:34.980
Makes sense.

14:35.420 --> 14:39.840
If they bid truthfully, the competition is going to drive the price right up to that ceiling.

14:40.060 --> 14:42.460
The clearing price for the ad becomes $10.

14:43.060 --> 14:46.000
Which means the advertisers make zero profit on the transaction.

14:46.000 --> 14:46.640
Exactly.

14:47.100 --> 14:48.200
The value of the ad is $10.

14:48.500 --> 14:49.200
They pay $10.

14:49.580 --> 14:50.760
They basically break even.

14:51.420 --> 14:54.820
The platform, however, extracts the entire gross value of the placements.

14:55.040 --> 14:56.320
They capture all the surplus.

14:56.760 --> 14:59.060
The platform maximizes its revenue perfectly.

14:59.700 --> 15:03.880
But the real structural insight comes when we look at what happens when you introduce more competition.

15:04.420 --> 15:05.160
Adding more bidders.

15:05.440 --> 15:05.600
Yeah.

15:06.460 --> 15:09.780
The essay analyzes the effect of adding new entrants to the auction.

15:09.780 --> 15:16.180
It proves mathematically that adding an entrant to the auction weakly raises the price of being seen.

15:16.900 --> 15:21.100
And weakly lowers the surplus for the incumbent advertisers who were already participating.

15:21.820 --> 15:21.840
Okay.

15:21.880 --> 15:27.240
Just to clarify, weakly in economic terms just means the price either goes up or stays the exact same.

15:27.340 --> 15:28.420
It never ever goes down.

15:28.620 --> 15:29.260
That's the baseline.

15:29.460 --> 15:29.660
Yes.

15:29.660 --> 15:34.220
But the mechanism that drives this upward price pressure is fascinating.

15:34.980 --> 15:36.380
And highly counterintuitive.

15:37.060 --> 15:37.420
How so?

15:38.020 --> 15:45.700
The model proves that a losing bidder, someone who buys nothing and gets zero ad placements, can actually dictate the price paid by the winner.

15:45.700 --> 15:49.480
This goes back to my silent charity auction analogy from the intro.

15:49.740 --> 15:52.880
How does a loser dictate the winner's reality in this digital system?

15:53.300 --> 15:58.100
Well, we need to understand that these platforms typically operate on a variation of a second-price auction.

15:58.500 --> 15:59.620
Let's simplify it.

15:59.680 --> 15:59.840
Okay.

16:00.440 --> 16:05.700
Imagine there is exactly one ad placement available in your feed right now.

16:06.040 --> 16:09.440
And we have three advertisers bidding for it in milliseconds.

16:10.720 --> 16:11.980
Advertiser A bids $10.

16:13.100 --> 16:14.620
Advertiser B bids $5.

16:14.620 --> 16:17.780
Advertiser C bids $3.

16:18.440 --> 16:18.720
Okay.

16:18.840 --> 16:23.040
So, Advertiser A clearly wins the spot because $10 is the highest bid.

16:23.680 --> 16:24.840
Advertiser A wins, yes.

16:25.700 --> 16:28.940
But in a second-price auction, they do not pay their own bid of $10.

16:29.600 --> 16:34.820
They pay the price of the highest losing bid, plus maybe a tiny fraction of a cent.

16:35.160 --> 16:35.720
Oh, right.

16:36.020 --> 16:42.540
So, Advertiser A wins the placement, but they only have to pay $5 because that was Advertiser B's bid.

16:42.880 --> 16:43.380
Got it.

16:43.380 --> 16:45.760
So, the winner pays the runner-up's price.

16:46.540 --> 16:47.540
Now, let's change the scenario.

16:48.500 --> 16:53.600
Let's imagine Advertiser B goes out of business, or they get banned from the platform for selling counterfeit goods.

16:54.280 --> 16:56.140
They are removed from the auction entirely.

16:56.700 --> 16:56.880
Okay.

16:57.320 --> 16:57.740
They're gone.

16:58.420 --> 17:02.360
Advertiser A still wins because their $10 bid is still the highest.

17:02.360 --> 17:04.200
But who's the runner-up now?

17:04.840 --> 17:08.200
The runner-up is Advertiser C, who only bid $3.

17:08.860 --> 17:12.480
So, Advertiser A's price just dropped from $5 to $3.

17:12.940 --> 17:16.140
Look at the incredible power of Advertiser B in that scenario.

17:16.800 --> 17:18.100
Advertiser B lost the auction.

17:18.680 --> 17:19.960
They got no ad placement.

17:20.480 --> 17:21.760
They acquired zero customers.

17:21.940 --> 17:22.100
Yeah.

17:22.100 --> 17:33.480
But simply by participating in the system, simply by existing and submitting a bid, they forced the actual winner to pay $2 more for the exact same placement.

17:33.480 --> 17:41.460
The guy holding the paddle at the charity auction, he didn't take the painting, but he made the winner bleed cash just by being in the room.

17:42.540 --> 17:47.100
This mathematical reality explains so much about the modern digital experience for small businesses.

17:47.480 --> 17:56.540
Like, when a local bakery or a boutique clothing store runs ads, why does it feel increasingly aggressive, volatile, and relentlessly expensive?

17:56.580 --> 17:57.280
Because it's rigged.

17:57.280 --> 18:02.940
Because you aren't just competing against the successful, highly profitable businesses who win the auctions.

18:03.600 --> 18:08.680
You're being financially squeezed by the sheer overwhelming volume of losers.

18:09.460 --> 18:09.980
Wow.

18:10.200 --> 18:19.820
Every single additional bidder that enters the platform, even if they have absolutely zero chance of winning a spot in the feed, acts as an upward pressure on the clearing price.

18:20.380 --> 18:26.120
They push the cost of advertising higher for the people who actually have viable, legitimate businesses to run.

18:26.380 --> 18:27.180
Wait, hold on.

18:27.280 --> 18:31.660
I want to push back on this a little because it feels like there's a logical cliff here.

18:32.180 --> 18:32.680
Go for it.

18:32.680 --> 18:41.680
If I'm an advertiser and I am consistently losing auctions, or worse, winning auctions but paying so much that I lose money on the actual sale, I'm going to go bankrupt.

18:41.900 --> 18:44.640
I am going to take my credit card off the platform and leave.

18:44.880 --> 18:45.560
Logically, yes.

18:45.880 --> 18:51.060
So the platform is kind of shooting itself in the foot by letting this hyperinflated environment persist, right?

18:51.240 --> 18:52.980
Eventually, they run out of suckers.

18:52.980 --> 18:57.480
The bad businesses die, the losers leave, and the prices should stabilize.

18:57.480 --> 19:04.360
Your logic is perfectly sound for a closed traditional market, but the digital ad market is not a closed system.

19:05.080 --> 19:13.780
The text introduces a concept called the steady state of replenishment, and it completely shatters the idea that the market will logically correct itself.

19:13.780 --> 19:14.940
Okay, let's hear it.

19:15.480 --> 19:16.480
How does the treadmill work?

19:16.620 --> 19:22.320
The author proposes a scenario where there is a constant, unending arrival of new entrants to the platform.

19:22.820 --> 19:29.280
These new entrants arrive believing that their expected profit margin from advertising is highly positive.

19:29.720 --> 19:30.400
They're optimistic.

19:30.400 --> 19:32.100
Highly optimistic.

19:32.560 --> 19:36.840
Let's say a guy sets up a dropshipping store selling cheap sunglasses.

19:37.340 --> 19:45.020
He reads a few marketing blogs and is utterly convinced that for every $5 he spends on ads, he will make $20 in sales.

19:45.140 --> 19:46.260
But the reality is different.

19:46.540 --> 19:53.920
The reality is his product is terrible, the market is saturated, and his actual expected margin is deeply negative.

19:54.480 --> 19:56.820
Every time his ad runs, he loses money.

19:57.160 --> 19:57.520
Ouch.

19:57.680 --> 19:59.980
Under normal circumstances, he would go bankrupt.

20:00.400 --> 20:01.840
And leave, as you said.

20:02.500 --> 20:06.320
But the model introduces the concept of turnover and replenishment.

20:06.940 --> 20:17.360
Before the market empties out, before the prices can drop back down to reality, a fresh wave of equally optimistic, equally misinformed new entrants arrives to take his place.

20:17.820 --> 20:21.140
Because there's a new guy starting a dropshipping store every five seconds.

20:21.560 --> 20:22.200
Exactly.

20:22.200 --> 20:29.960
Because this influx of new, optimistic money keeps happening, a bizarre mathematical equilibrium is reached.

20:30.400 --> 20:38.860
The platform is able to sell every single available ad placement at a positive price, generating massive, consistent profit for the platform.

20:39.160 --> 20:40.240
But simultaneously.

20:40.960 --> 20:45.520
Simultaneously, every single purchase yields a negative value for the buyer.

20:45.740 --> 20:47.040
That is mind-bending.

20:47.040 --> 20:55.060
Positive, record-breaking platform revenue coexisting perfectly with systemic, guaranteed negative value for the buyers.

20:55.360 --> 20:56.760
It's an endless treadmill.

20:57.500 --> 21:00.780
But where does this infinite supply of suckers actually come from?

21:00.780 --> 21:09.700
The author points to a deeply human reality, the sheer scale of human optimism, and the democratization of creation tools.

21:10.160 --> 21:11.480
Think about the modern internet.

21:11.860 --> 21:20.020
It has never been easier to write a self-published book, record a song in your bedroom, design a t-shirt, or set up an e-commerce storefront.

21:20.020 --> 21:24.420
The barrier to entry for creating a single product is practically zero.

21:24.940 --> 21:29.760
And the second you finish that product, what does every single piece of advice on the internet tell you?

21:30.100 --> 21:30.860
Promote it.

21:31.020 --> 21:31.780
Run a campaign.

21:32.260 --> 21:33.060
Boost this post.

21:33.500 --> 21:34.380
Find your audience.

21:35.040 --> 21:45.560
There is a massive structural gap between what these millions of individuals believe the value of an ad impression is and the actual cold economic reality of the market.

21:45.560 --> 21:47.500
So they just dive in blindly.

21:48.260 --> 21:56.500
There is a practically infinite supply of humans creating their very first product and acting on the universally accepted advice to buy advertising.

21:57.140 --> 22:02.200
This endless replenishing supply of hope keeps demand perpetually above capacity.

22:02.660 --> 22:03.700
Even if they churn and burn.

22:04.220 --> 22:13.020
Even if every single one of those individuals eventually burns through their savings and churns out of the system, there are 10 more arriving tomorrow to take their place.

22:13.360 --> 22:15.220
It is quite literally a tax on hope.

22:15.220 --> 22:16.520
That's exactly what it is.

22:16.760 --> 22:21.220
The platform is monetizing the optimism of amateurs to drive up the cost for professionals.

22:21.600 --> 22:25.260
But if that's true, why wouldn't the platform just clean up the ecosystem?

22:26.000 --> 22:26.420
What do you mean?

22:26.840 --> 22:35.200
Like if they know a business is unviable, if they know an advertiser is basically running a scam or a doomed dropshipping store, why not just ban them?

22:35.860 --> 22:39.780
Wouldn't a cleaner, more reputable marketplace be better for the platform long term?

22:40.180 --> 22:43.060
Think about the math we just discussed with the second price auction.

22:43.060 --> 22:47.940
Let's use this scale analogy to explain the platform's internal screening calculus.

22:48.440 --> 22:50.440
Okay, I'm visualizing a scale.

22:50.940 --> 22:56.440
On one side of the scale, the platform has the long-term benefits of banning a spammy or unviable business.

22:56.680 --> 23:02.120
They save the hard cost of paying human moderators or running server space to review these bad ads.

23:02.360 --> 23:04.840
They avoid some minor brand damage.

23:04.840 --> 23:07.620
Okay, so that's the incentive to clean up.

23:07.780 --> 23:09.060
What's on the other side of the scale?

23:09.720 --> 23:13.760
On the other side of the scale is the immediate revenue the platform loses by taking action.

23:14.020 --> 23:17.740
But it is not just the loss of the spammers' specific ad budget.

23:18.200 --> 23:20.500
Remember the guy holding the paddle at the silent auction?

23:20.840 --> 23:21.440
Oh, right.

23:21.920 --> 23:22.680
The runner-up.

23:22.680 --> 23:29.260
If the platform bans that unviable business and kicks them out of the room, they remove a losing bid from millions of micro-auctions.

23:29.980 --> 23:34.320
When you remove the runner-up, what happens to the price paid by the winner?

23:34.460 --> 23:35.240
The price drops.

23:35.620 --> 23:35.840
Yeah.

23:35.880 --> 23:36.720
The winner pays less.

23:37.240 --> 23:42.100
The mathematics prove that the revenue lost by removing the upward price pressure of the spammer

23:42.100 --> 23:45.580
almost always outweighs the operational benefit of banning them.

23:46.200 --> 23:49.900
The platform literally loses money by cleaning up the ecosystem.

23:50.000 --> 23:50.840
That's insane.

23:50.840 --> 23:56.540
They have a mathematically weak structural incentive to screen for actual business viability.

23:57.200 --> 24:02.380
So they let the garbage stay in the system because the garbage makes the good businesses pay higher rent.

24:02.700 --> 24:03.000
Exactly.

24:03.640 --> 24:08.940
They will aggressively screen for things that threaten their immediate legal standing or their core delivery mechanics,

24:09.540 --> 24:15.460
like glaring policy violations, illegal content, or ads that users physically cannot close.

24:15.800 --> 24:17.160
Sure, the stuff that gets them sued.

24:17.160 --> 24:23.900
But they will not heavily screen for whether an advertiser's business model is actually profitable or culturally valuable.

24:24.560 --> 24:29.860
As long as the bid clears, the quality of the business is irrelevant to the platform's bottom line.

24:29.860 --> 24:34.680
This paints such a bleak picture of what we are actually scrolling through every day.

24:34.820 --> 24:41.200
I mean, we have a feed flooded with a mix of organic content that somehow managed to survive the algorithmic chokehold

24:41.200 --> 24:49.960
and paid content that is being driven by an infinite replenishing treadmill of unviable businesses artificially inflating the market.

24:50.060 --> 24:50.980
It's a potent mix.

24:50.980 --> 24:54.280
Which brings me to a huge psychological question.

24:54.680 --> 24:58.940
How does this toxic mixture actually warp our perception of reality?

24:59.540 --> 25:04.140
How does it affect what we as users perceive as good or valuable or popular?

25:04.780 --> 25:09.780
The author dedicates a significant portion of the analysis to this exact psychological impact.

25:10.280 --> 25:13.300
It's fundamentally about the dilution of discrimination.

25:13.300 --> 25:19.220
I want to translate that phrase, dilution of discrimination, because it sounds very academic.

25:19.660 --> 25:23.300
It basically means the feed is breaking our social compass, right?

25:23.700 --> 25:29.780
It's watering down our ability to tell what is actually culturally resonant from what is just heavily funded.

25:30.140 --> 25:31.740
That is an excellent translation.

25:32.540 --> 25:35.460
Think about what an exposure in your feed actually signifies.

25:36.280 --> 25:39.240
The essay separates exposure into two distinct routes.

25:39.900 --> 25:42.440
Organic selection and paid selection.

25:42.440 --> 25:43.940
Let's break those down.

25:44.460 --> 25:45.160
Organic first.

25:45.700 --> 25:52.360
Organic selection is correlated, imperfectly, but correlated, with humans actually valuing an item.

25:53.220 --> 26:00.020
A video or an article spreads organically because people watched it, laughed at it, were outraged by it,

26:00.420 --> 26:02.180
shared it with their friends, or commented on it.

26:02.400 --> 26:04.420
It requires a chain of human reaction.

26:04.760 --> 26:05.140
Exactly.

26:05.500 --> 26:09.720
It is a signal, however noisy, of genuine human interest.

26:09.720 --> 26:12.960
It has to earn its place in the feed through resonance.

26:13.440 --> 26:14.280
What about the paid route?

26:14.880 --> 26:20.100
Paid selection is correlated with one thing, a producer's willingness and ability to pay.

26:20.860 --> 26:22.160
Now, we have to be fair here.

26:22.600 --> 26:24.880
Paid selection isn't completely uninformative.

26:25.260 --> 26:25.440
True.

26:25.560 --> 26:26.940
A good product can still be advertised.

26:27.660 --> 26:27.920
Right.

26:28.180 --> 26:34.180
A company willing to spend $10,000 a day on ads might actually have a fantastic, revolutionary new product.

26:34.180 --> 26:38.540
But it is a fundamentally different signal than organic virality.

26:38.900 --> 26:39.660
Yeah, totally different.

26:40.300 --> 26:49.280
The problem arises when you mix these two completely different signals together, in a single, endlessly scrolling feed without stark, unmissable boundaries.

26:49.680 --> 26:51.580
Because money becomes the great equalizer.

26:52.180 --> 26:54.300
It counterfeits the appearance of popularity.

26:54.840 --> 26:57.860
Let's say organic selection has a very high quality threshold.

26:57.860 --> 27:04.060
A truly amazing piece of content is a thousand times more likely to be shown organically than a terrible piece of content.

27:04.520 --> 27:08.600
The gap between high quality and low quality is vast.

27:09.680 --> 27:12.620
But in the paid route, that gap shrinks dramatically.

27:13.060 --> 27:16.280
If you have a credit card, your terrible product will be seen.

27:16.540 --> 27:19.020
Money forces exposure, regardless of quality.

27:19.020 --> 27:27.840
So as the feed injects more and more paid placements, the overall quality of what I'm looking at gets dragged down to the lowest common denominator of whoever is willing to pay.

27:28.720 --> 27:33.500
The feed's overall ability to discriminate between high and low quality collapses.

27:33.860 --> 27:34.960
The signal is diluted.

27:35.900 --> 27:43.940
But the paper makes a really crucial distinction here between the overall state of the feed and how you, the individual user, process that information in your brain.

27:44.260 --> 27:45.860
Right, because I'm not totally helpless.

27:46.340 --> 27:48.240
If I see an ad, I generally know it's an ad.

27:48.240 --> 27:52.300
You use what the author calls a likelihood ratio to judge a post.

27:52.900 --> 27:59.420
If you're scrolling and you spot a tiny gray sponsored label under a brand's name, your brain instantly updates its assumptions.

27:59.860 --> 28:00.500
You discount it.

28:00.960 --> 28:02.860
You know that post arrived via the paid route.

28:03.480 --> 28:05.540
You instinctively discount its popularity.

28:06.240 --> 28:08.120
You know it's in your feed because someone bought the space.

28:08.580 --> 28:11.020
Not necessarily because it is universally loved.

28:11.020 --> 28:17.660
But that psychological defense mechanism entirely relies on me actually seeing and registering the label.

28:18.240 --> 28:23.840
And platforms are notorious for making those labels as subtle and easily missed as legally possible.

28:24.060 --> 28:24.840
They absolutely are.

28:25.420 --> 28:27.740
But even beyond the label, what about the metrics themselves?

28:27.980 --> 28:29.340
What about the engagement counts?

28:29.660 --> 28:32.700
This is perhaps the most insidious part of the signal dilution.

28:32.700 --> 28:35.160
Consider an engagement count.

28:35.520 --> 28:40.840
A little counter at the bottom of a video that says 1 million views or a post with 100,000 likes.

28:40.980 --> 28:41.100
Right.

28:41.660 --> 28:53.240
If that metric does not explicitly break down the traffic source, if it doesn't separate the organic reach from the paid reach, it is conflating two completely different phenomena into one single number.

28:53.240 --> 28:53.680
Exactly.

28:54.300 --> 28:58.860
When I look at a post with a million views, my social compass immediately fires off.

28:58.960 --> 29:02.940
My brain says, wow, a million human beings cared about this enough to watch it.

29:03.160 --> 29:04.360
This is culturally important.

29:04.520 --> 29:05.820
You assume organic resonance.

29:06.200 --> 29:17.480
But because of the paid route, I literally cannot know if it's there because it's organically amazing or because a mediocre brand had a massive marketing budget and brute-forced those million impressions.

29:18.020 --> 29:20.200
The view metric has been completely hijacked.

29:20.200 --> 29:23.200
It breaks your ability to judge collective interest.

29:23.900 --> 29:26.000
And it doesn't just deceive the user.

29:26.640 --> 29:28.520
It deceives the producers themselves.

29:28.960 --> 29:29.300
How so?

29:30.020 --> 29:38.200
A small business owner might run an ad campaign, look at their dashboard, see a massive spike in likes and views, and conclude that their new marketing angle is brilliant.

29:38.900 --> 29:41.460
In reality, their content wasn't any better.

29:42.080 --> 29:45.000
They simply paid the platform to shove it in front of more eyeballs.

29:45.000 --> 29:52.680
They conflate the volume of purchased exposure with an actual genuine human response rate.

29:53.160 --> 30:00.420
The entire ecosystem is flying blind, relying on a compass where the magnetic north is just whoever spent the most money that day.

30:00.420 --> 30:04.520
Okay, so we've talked about the platform extracting all the revenue.

30:05.040 --> 30:10.940
We've talked about the advertisers running on this brutal treadmill, losing money but driving up prices for everyone else.

30:11.640 --> 30:15.160
And we've talked about how the metrics deceive both sides.

30:15.400 --> 30:19.280
But we have to turn to the third party in this transaction, the silent party.

30:19.520 --> 30:21.920
The most important party in the entire equation.

30:22.780 --> 30:23.940
You, the listener.

30:23.940 --> 30:29.780
This brings us to a concept that is so central to our daily lives, yet so rarely quantified.

30:30.500 --> 30:32.800
The pricing of our attention.

30:33.380 --> 30:39.460
Because in all this sophisticated math about bids and clearing prices and auctions happening in milliseconds,

30:40.000 --> 30:44.940
the person who actually has to look at the ad isn't even factored into the equation.

30:44.940 --> 30:51.900
That is the core revelation regarding what an algorithmically bid-ranked allocation completely ignores.

30:51.900 --> 30:54.140
Let's look at the actual transaction.

30:55.180 --> 31:02.440
Money changes hands between the entrant, the advertiser who pays the bill, and the platform, the auctioneer who collects the cash.

31:02.920 --> 31:03.020
Right.

31:03.260 --> 31:05.820
The transaction is entirely between those two entities.

31:06.300 --> 31:09.080
But I'm the one sitting on the couch who has my feed interrupted.

31:09.460 --> 31:11.700
I'm the one who has to process the distraction.

31:12.360 --> 31:12.700
Exactly.

31:13.180 --> 31:17.900
The author introduces a variable representing the attention cost paid entirely by the recipient.

31:17.900 --> 31:24.880
Every single time an unwanted, irrelevant, or annoying ad is injected into your feed, it exacts a toll.

31:25.360 --> 31:25.900
A real toll.

31:26.440 --> 31:27.880
It costs you cognitive effort.

31:28.300 --> 31:29.180
It costs you time.

31:29.600 --> 31:30.740
It disrupts your focus.

31:31.540 --> 31:35.480
You, the user, bear the entirety of that physical and psychological cost.

31:35.680 --> 31:40.340
The transaction happens completely over my head, but I'm the one paying the toll on the road.

31:40.340 --> 31:48.140
The mathematical models prove that an auction designed to maximize the sum of the bids for the platform takes absolutely no account of the recipient's value.

31:48.600 --> 31:56.920
If an advertiser forces a terrible, loud, irrelevant video into your feed, your expected value from that exposure is mathematically negative.

31:57.540 --> 31:59.280
It actively harms your experience.

31:59.800 --> 32:06.360
Because it's annoying, it distracts me from the news or the friends I actually want to see, and I get zero utility from the product being sold.

32:06.360 --> 32:11.360
Furthermore, the platform's allocation model ignores the valuation error of the advertiser.

32:11.940 --> 32:15.660
Remember the optimistic dropshipper who is losing money on every click?

32:15.740 --> 32:15.940
Yeah.

32:16.760 --> 32:17.680
The platform doesn't care.

32:18.440 --> 32:30.740
So we arrive at a systemic outcome where the platform maximizes its revenue, the advertiser consistently loses money on a bad campaign, and you, the user, suffer a negative attention cost.

32:30.740 --> 32:37.420
The overall welfare of the entire system completely diverges from the sheer monetary value of the bids.

32:38.000 --> 32:39.540
So what does this all mean for us practically?

32:40.340 --> 32:49.460
It means that you are subsidizing this entire digital economy with your uncompensated attention, and you have absolutely no voice in the auction itself.

32:49.640 --> 32:51.940
You cannot bid money against an unwanted placement.

32:51.940 --> 33:00.740
If I see an ad for a mobile game that just makes my blood boil, I can't reach into my wallet and pay the platform $5 to outbid the advertiser and make the ad disappear.

33:01.500 --> 33:04.480
The platform does not allow you to price your own attention and currency.

33:05.100 --> 33:12.060
The only currency you are allowed to spend to signal your displeasure, to signal a negative value, is your own manual labor.

33:12.060 --> 33:21.780
Taking the time, the physical clicks, and the cognitive effort to open the little submenu on the post, find the right option, and hit hide or block or report this ad.

33:22.120 --> 33:27.940
The system externalizes its own quality control by pricing it in the recipient's physical and mental labor.

33:28.440 --> 33:30.380
You have to clean up their mess for free.

33:30.560 --> 33:34.560
Which naturally leads to the question of how effective that manual labor actually is.

33:34.560 --> 33:41.800
Because in my personal experience, and I'm sure every listener can relate to this, clicking block feels like tossing a pebble into the ocean.

33:42.260 --> 33:44.320
It feels completely, utterly futile.

33:44.940 --> 33:46.680
Your frustration is not just anecdotal.

33:47.000 --> 33:49.660
It is mathematically validated by the source text.

33:50.400 --> 33:56.960
The author explores the economics of avoidance costs and what is fundamentally a massive whack-a-mole problem.

33:57.200 --> 33:58.200
Whack-a-mole, exactly.

33:58.800 --> 34:04.220
The analysis proves that no finite sequence of entity-level hides is ever durable.

34:04.220 --> 34:06.520
Okay, let's translate entity-level hides.

34:06.640 --> 34:07.920
What does that mean in plain English?

34:08.760 --> 34:17.580
When you see a garbage ad and you click block, you are telling the platform to block that specific exact page or that specific ad account.

34:17.840 --> 34:19.620
You are blocking the entity.

34:19.960 --> 34:20.080
Right.

34:20.300 --> 34:26.140
But the person sitting behind a computer running that page, the operator, is completely unaffected.

34:26.260 --> 34:26.500
Right.

34:26.780 --> 34:29.560
I spent an hour last week trying to clean up my feed.

34:29.560 --> 34:34.880
I kept getting these identical ads for cheap, drop-shipping t-shirts with stolen artwork.

34:35.160 --> 34:37.160
I blocked a page called Awesome Tees.

34:37.440 --> 34:47.020
I refreshed my feed, and five minutes later, the exact same t-shirt with the exact same stolen artwork popped up in an ad from a page called CoolTees99.

34:47.020 --> 34:49.120
It is absolutely infuriating.

34:49.720 --> 34:52.740
That is the structural vulnerability of entity-level controls.

34:53.180 --> 35:02.660
If an operator can continually and cheaply replace excluded entities, and your block does not automatically apply to their new creations, then you will inevitably be exposed again.

35:03.060 --> 35:03.840
You can't win.

35:03.840 --> 35:13.220
The math proves that no finite number of clicks by you, no matter how much manual labor you expend, will ever guarantee durable avoidance of that operator's content.

35:13.760 --> 35:15.220
It's the ultimate game of whack-a-mole.

35:15.780 --> 35:20.980
I hit one, and another one pops up instantly because the barrier to creating a new page is zero.

35:21.580 --> 35:25.760
The paper actually includes a really revealing anecdote about a user trying to fight this, right?

35:26.060 --> 35:30.260
It does, and it illustrates the sheer scale of the labor tax imposed on users.

35:30.260 --> 35:36.700
The author highlights a self-reported blocked list kept over several years by a single highly motivated user.

35:37.320 --> 35:40.540
This list features thousands of distinct entries.

35:41.440 --> 35:41.680
Thousands.

35:42.140 --> 35:50.020
The user reported that just to maintain a baseline acceptable experience, they were having to manually block on the order of 100 new entities per visit.

35:50.320 --> 35:52.560
100 manual blocks per session.

35:52.800 --> 35:56.760
That is an insane amount of labor just to make the app usable.

35:56.760 --> 36:01.560
It's like having to pick up 100 pieces of trash in a park before you're allowed to sit on a bench.

36:02.400 --> 36:06.380
And the most telling detail from that anecdote involves a single legitimate publisher.

36:07.260 --> 36:14.780
This publisher branded themselves under five separate pages, four city-specific pages, and one main regional page.

36:14.920 --> 36:15.080
Okay.

36:15.680 --> 36:21.600
The user found the content irrelevant and had to manually block every single one of those five pages individually.

36:21.600 --> 36:27.700
A business operating in the user's own province required its own specific block.

36:28.020 --> 36:31.440
It demonstrates that the core issue isn't even just international scammers.

36:31.980 --> 36:36.500
It's the simple reality that a single operator can infinitely fragment their identity.

36:37.160 --> 36:38.540
So this is where I get really confused.

36:38.900 --> 36:42.540
Why can't the platform just see it's the same guy with the same credit card?

36:42.540 --> 36:49.600
If I block AwesomeTease, why doesn't the platform use its massive, omniscient back-end data to say,

36:50.080 --> 36:55.080
ah, the guy running AwesomeTease also owns CoolTease99 and 50 other pages.

36:55.240 --> 36:57.540
Let's block them all for this user instantly.

36:57.980 --> 36:59.300
Why not block the operator?

36:59.760 --> 37:02.340
That is the critical question of operator-level control.

37:02.780 --> 37:04.140
Why not ban the parent?

37:04.760 --> 37:08.820
To understand the platform's reluctance, we have to look at the math of attribution.

37:08.820 --> 37:15.600
Attribution is the challenge of correctly linking a new, unknown page to a known, blocked operator.

37:16.300 --> 37:17.320
They have to connect the dots.

37:18.020 --> 37:24.040
When a platform builds an automated rule to connect those dots, that rule will inevitably make two kinds of errors.

37:24.560 --> 37:26.160
False positives and false negatives.

37:26.500 --> 37:29.760
It catches the wrong guy, or it lets the bad guy slip through.

37:30.380 --> 37:31.820
Consider a giant scale again.

37:32.460 --> 37:37.700
On one side, you have the recall rate, how effectively the rule catches the spammer's new pages.

37:37.700 --> 37:47.880
On the other side, you have the false grouping rate, the probability that the algorithm accidentally blocks a completely innocent, unrelated publisher that you actually wanted to see.

37:48.460 --> 37:56.040
Like it accidentally blocks my favorite local bakery because they happen to use the same web hosting service as the spammer, or they share a keyword in their name.

37:56.360 --> 37:56.820
Exactly.

37:57.520 --> 38:03.900
If a platform institutes a heavy-handed, operator-level block, the risk of false grouping skyrockets.

38:03.900 --> 38:16.920
A mathematical threshold dictates that an operator-level control only actually improves the user's experience if the benefit of catching the spam is strictly greater than the agonizing cost of losing content you actually love.

38:17.440 --> 38:17.960
That makes sense.

38:18.760 --> 38:29.240
A crude rule that catches 99% of the dropshipping scams but accidentally blocks half of the news and creators you genuinely care about makes your feed demonstrably worse.

38:29.240 --> 38:36.440
But hold on. Let's take that a step further. We're talking about tech companies that know our locations, our browsing habits, our friend groups.

38:36.920 --> 38:41.120
They have account-level data. They don't have to guess based on keywords.

38:41.760 --> 38:43.640
They know who pays the server bills.

38:43.840 --> 38:44.000
They do.

38:44.420 --> 38:46.720
They know who the shared admins are on the back end.

38:46.800 --> 38:50.940
They literally have the operator's credit card on file to charge them for the ads.

38:50.940 --> 38:58.660
So they could enforce operator-level blocking perfectly with zero false positives using their back-end financial data.

38:58.720 --> 38:59.560
So why don't they?

38:59.920 --> 39:04.080
This is where we uncover the darkest structural incentive in the entire architecture.

39:04.860 --> 39:09.400
The answer goes all the way back to our discussion of the auction and the guy holding the paddle.

39:09.920 --> 39:12.220
Oh, the losing bidder sets the price.

39:12.640 --> 39:16.180
Consider the alternative if they implement perfect operator-level blocking.

39:16.180 --> 39:19.600
Let's say the spammer has 50 alternative pages.

39:20.960 --> 39:26.160
Even if those 50 pages never actually win an ad placement in your feed because their bids are low,

39:27.100 --> 39:32.960
simply by existing and participating in the auction for your attention, they are acting as upward price pressure.

39:33.220 --> 39:34.000
Oh, my God.

39:34.160 --> 39:38.260
They are driving up the clearing price for the legitimate advertisers who do win.

39:38.440 --> 39:38.720
Right.

39:38.720 --> 39:45.260
If the platform builds a perfect credit card-based operator block, and the moment you block awesome tees,

39:45.500 --> 39:49.720
all 50 of that spammer's alt pages are instantly nuked from your personal auction pool,

39:50.220 --> 39:53.200
the platform immediately loses that upward price pressure.

39:53.880 --> 39:55.960
The density of the auction decreases.

39:56.880 --> 40:00.600
And when auction density decreases, the platform's revenue drops.

40:00.600 --> 40:06.640
So they deliberately offer me a weak, ineffective blocking tool because my suffering is profitable.

40:07.300 --> 40:11.800
A functioning block button would literally cost them millions of dollars in inflated ad revenue.

40:12.320 --> 40:19.240
The platform has a deep, mathematically sound structural incentive to offer weaker avoidance controls than the user would prefer.

40:19.800 --> 40:21.380
It isn't a conspiracy theory.

40:21.560 --> 40:29.100
It is simply the logical, inevitable outcome of running a second-price auction fueled by an infinite supply of low-quality bidders.

40:29.100 --> 40:31.440
That is insane.

40:32.180 --> 40:37.660
Catching the spammer reduces auction density, and density is the lifeblood of their profit margin.

40:38.020 --> 40:41.980
That is deeply cynical, but the math is undeniable.

40:42.400 --> 40:47.620
It's structural extraction operating invisibly behind a friendly blue thumbs-up button.

40:48.340 --> 40:51.800
Which brings us to the final and most constructive part of the analysis.

40:52.320 --> 40:57.980
If the architecture inherently favors platform control, if it dilutes our social signals,

40:57.980 --> 41:02.980
and if it traps users in an endless cycle of uncompensated whack-a-mole labor,

41:03.880 --> 41:05.240
what are the actual solutions?

41:06.000 --> 41:09.920
What structural remedies does the author propose to fix the math?

41:10.480 --> 41:16.600
The author outlines four specific, structurally sound remedies designed to dismantle these asymmetric power dynamics.

41:17.000 --> 41:18.460
Let's walk through them logically.

41:18.960 --> 41:21.980
The first remedy addresses the access side of the equation.

41:22.400 --> 41:23.700
The illusion of the follow.

41:23.700 --> 41:28.400
The remedy is guaranteed delivery for opted-in followers.

41:29.040 --> 41:31.440
This sounds like a return to the chronological feed.

41:31.900 --> 41:35.420
If I click follow on a creator, I am guaranteed to see their post.

41:35.620 --> 41:37.020
The algorithm doesn't get a veto.

41:37.580 --> 41:41.220
It restores the follow relationship to what the user originally intended.

41:41.820 --> 41:43.800
A direct, binding connection.

41:43.800 --> 41:49.900
Now, the text acknowledges the primary counter-argument to this, which the platforms use constantly,

41:50.140 --> 41:52.120
which is the problem of feed volume.

41:52.780 --> 41:52.960
Right.

41:53.040 --> 41:56.800
If you follow a thousand highly active pages and they all post five times a day,

41:56.880 --> 42:00.280
your feed would become an unusable firehose of content.

42:00.660 --> 42:00.860
True.

42:01.080 --> 42:04.680
A pure chronological feed of a thousand accounts would be overwhelming.

42:04.680 --> 42:11.600
So, any guarantee of delivery would need to be paired with intelligent user tuning, or frequency rules.

42:12.240 --> 42:12.640
Like what?

42:13.080 --> 42:19.420
Perhaps you categorize your follows into tiers, or you set limits on how many times a day a brand can ping you.

42:19.960 --> 42:21.760
But the core principle remains.

42:22.300 --> 42:27.080
The user's explicit choice must override the platform's algorithmic chokehold.

42:27.560 --> 42:28.860
That makes total sense.

42:29.160 --> 42:30.500
Give the control back to the user.

42:30.840 --> 42:31.900
What is the second remedy?

42:31.900 --> 42:36.740
The second remedy is the disclosure of delivery routes and reach breakdowns.

42:37.260 --> 42:41.800
This is aimed directly at fixing the signal dilution and the broken social compass we discussed earlier.

42:42.140 --> 42:44.340
Fixing the engagement count conflation.

42:44.700 --> 42:45.020
Exactly.

42:45.540 --> 42:53.840
For the user interface, it means implementing clear, unambiguous, standardized labels showing exactly how an item reached you.

42:54.340 --> 42:56.900
No more tiny gray text that blends into the background.

42:57.340 --> 43:01.280
It needs to be painfully obvious if a post is organic or purchased.

43:01.280 --> 43:02.780
And what about for the creators?

43:03.500 --> 43:11.000
Even more importantly, for the producers, it means analytics dashboards that definitively separate organic reach from page reach.

43:11.400 --> 43:13.220
A creator needs to see the denominator.

43:13.680 --> 43:20.700
They need to know, okay, 10,000 people saw this because they loved it, and 50,000 saw it because I paid for it.

43:20.960 --> 43:24.540
Without that separation, they are just lying to themselves about their own popularity.

43:24.540 --> 43:28.980
It gives producers the ability to compute real human response rates.

43:29.080 --> 43:35.340
Rather than just staring at a bloated metric inflated by their own ad spend, it restores reality to the metrics.

43:36.140 --> 43:39.040
Remedy number three tackles the whack-a-mole problem.

43:39.420 --> 43:41.100
Operator level avoidance controls.

43:41.100 --> 43:46.460
The scope of the user's control needs to seamlessly match the scope of the operator's exposure.

43:47.180 --> 43:56.020
If a bad actor spins up 50 pages, a single block from a user should sever the relationship with the human being running the operation, not just the throwaway page name.

43:56.420 --> 44:02.040
And as we discussed, we know the platforms have the back-end financial data to do this accurately without false positives.

44:02.440 --> 44:06.460
They just have to be forced to prioritize user experience over auction density.

44:06.460 --> 44:07.460
Precisely.

44:08.000 --> 44:16.920
And finally, the fourth remedy, which is perhaps the most profound structural shift proposed in the entire essay, portability of the follower graph.

44:17.600 --> 44:18.200
Portability.

44:18.740 --> 44:21.740
The ability to pack up your audience and leave the platform entirely.

44:22.300 --> 44:25.300
Let's consider the alternate model, something like an email newsletter.

44:26.020 --> 44:34.160
If you spend five years building an email list of 50,000 subscribers, you have a direct, stable identifier for every single one of those followers.

44:34.500 --> 44:35.540
You own the list.

44:35.540 --> 44:35.800
Right.

44:36.460 --> 44:45.200
If your current email service provider suddenly doubles their prices or decides they don't like your content, you can export that CSV file of email addresses.

44:45.200 --> 44:51.040
You can move to a competitor, plug the list in, and send an email saying, hey, everyone, I've moved.

44:51.400 --> 44:52.880
You have the ultimate leverage.

44:53.700 --> 44:55.060
You have the option of exit.

44:55.720 --> 45:00.500
But on a social media platform, the platform holds the graph hostage.

45:01.120 --> 45:04.480
You do not have your followers' emails or direct contact information.

45:04.480 --> 45:10.220
If the platform's algorithm decides tomorrow that your reach is zero, you are completely trapped.

45:10.840 --> 45:12.040
You can't even tell them you're leaving.

45:12.620 --> 45:20.220
You cannot even send a message to your followers telling them to find you on a different app because the algorithm simply won't show them that message.

45:20.780 --> 45:25.620
The platform's entire competitive advantage relies on keeping you locked in the basement of the house you built.

45:25.620 --> 45:35.920
Portability would mandate that producers have the right to export their follower relationships with cryptographic user consent, of course, and move them to competing delivery systems.

45:35.920 --> 45:40.040
It would restore the fundamental market power of exit to the creator.

45:40.780 --> 45:48.560
I want to play devil's advocate here because the author anticipates pushback, and I can hear platform executives screaming these objections already.

45:49.220 --> 45:50.180
Let's tackle them.

45:51.080 --> 45:51.780
Objection one.

45:52.620 --> 45:54.740
Doesn't advertising fund a free service?

45:55.240 --> 46:02.420
If you mess with our auction density or mandate guaranteed reach, you will destroy the revenue model that keeps the internet free for billions of people.

46:02.420 --> 46:07.300
The author's rebuttal to that is purely mathematical, based on the auction models we discussed.

46:07.780 --> 46:11.120
Yes, advertising revenue funds the servers and the free service.

46:11.620 --> 46:19.160
But acknowledging that fact does not answer the critical economic question of who captures the gross surplus value of the interactions.

46:19.920 --> 46:20.760
The platform does.

46:20.760 --> 46:31.860
The benchmark auction model clearly demonstrates that the current competitive allocation moves almost all of the surplus value away from the businesses and the users directly into the pockets of the platform.

46:32.920 --> 46:39.440
The platform is extracting maximum possible rent far beyond what is necessary to simply fund a free service.

46:39.600 --> 46:39.740
Okay.

46:40.060 --> 46:40.660
Objection two.

46:41.000 --> 46:43.440
What about the small businesses, the mom and pop shops?

46:43.840 --> 46:46.240
Don't they rely on targeted ads to survive?

46:46.580 --> 46:49.300
If you restrict the system, you are crushing the little guy.

46:49.300 --> 46:58.880
The mathematical model concedes that some early adopters or highly niche businesses might initially benefit, but remember the comparative statics of adding bidders.

46:59.660 --> 47:06.060
As the platform aggressively recruits more advertisers, the surplus for the incumbent small businesses weekly falls.

47:06.860 --> 47:07.160
Right.

47:07.320 --> 47:07.760
The treadmill.

47:08.000 --> 47:11.200
The entire ecosystem relies on the replenishment treadmill.

47:11.200 --> 47:15.480
The system does not function by guaranteeing the success of small businesses.

47:16.060 --> 47:23.620
It functions by churning through an infinite supply of over-optimistic entrepreneurs who lose their savings to fill the platform's ad capacity.

47:24.620 --> 47:31.160
The platform thrives on high turnover and high volume, not on the individual viability or success of the little guy.

47:31.160 --> 47:38.300
So, bringing this all together after an hour of dissecting the math, what is the ultimate practical takeaway for the listener going about their day?

47:38.740 --> 47:41.140
It all distills down to a very simple concept.

47:41.860 --> 47:43.360
The persistence of your choices.

47:44.520 --> 47:50.520
In the physical world, in the architecture of a real house, if you close a door and lock it, it stays closed.

47:51.200 --> 47:54.240
If you invite a friend inside, they step into your living room.

47:54.240 --> 47:57.120
The physical world respects your autonomy.

47:57.980 --> 47:59.060
And the digital world doesn't.

47:59.380 --> 48:04.180
Well, the digital world respects your yes when you click follow and your no when you click block.

48:05.100 --> 48:14.560
Currently, the structural math proves beyond a shadow of a doubt that the platform respects neither of your choices the moment those choices conflict with their algorithmic revenue optimization and their auction density.

48:14.560 --> 48:26.080
The digital architecture we live in is explicitly designed to auction your attention to the highest bidder, to dilute your social signals so you can't tell what's real, and to tax you with endless manual labor to clean up their mess.

48:26.440 --> 48:31.060
All while keeping the creators and businesses you actually love running on a treadmill of rented access.

48:31.460 --> 48:35.460
It is, in a dark way, a masterpiece of structural extraction.

48:35.880 --> 48:41.620
A masterpiece of extraction entirely hidden behind a frictionless, friendly, infinitely scrolling interface.

48:41.960 --> 48:43.560
Which brings me to a final thought.

48:43.560 --> 48:45.780
I haven't been able to shake since reading this.

48:46.260 --> 48:52.500
We have spent this entire deep dive looking at the math and the architecture as it exists right now, today.

48:53.160 --> 49:03.760
And the core driver of this problem, as we saw with the replenishment treadmill, is that there is an infinite supply of optimistic humans creating single products and flooding the paid auction.

49:04.720 --> 49:10.540
But it takes time for a human to write a bad e-book, or record a bad song, or set up a dropshipping store.

49:10.540 --> 49:14.700
There is a natural, physical friction to human production.

49:15.260 --> 49:16.620
It slows the treadmill down.

49:17.140 --> 49:29.040
But what happens in the next three to five years when AI agents can automatically generate infinite products and simultaneously generate infinite ad campaigns and bids in these exact same auctions?

49:29.040 --> 49:43.340
If the platform's revenue thrives on the sheer overwhelming volume of bidders driving up the clearing price, an autonomous AI-driven auction is going to cause the attention cost, that psychological toll you pay, to absolutely skyrocket.

49:43.340 --> 49:48.380
We might be moving toward a feed that is an entirely machine-to-machine economy.

49:49.060 --> 49:59.080
AI agents generating products, AI bidding against other AI on ad space, platform algorithms selling the space, and human attention is no longer the target.

49:59.720 --> 50:03.120
Human attention just becomes the exhaust fumes of a machine economy.

50:03.120 --> 50:06.820
That is a chilling, but logically sound extrapolation of the math.

50:07.820 --> 50:21.660
If the supply of bidders transitions from optimistic humans with limited time to autonomous AI with zero marginal cost of production, the clearing prices will detach entirely from human economic reality.

50:22.160 --> 50:23.300
The treadmill speeds up.

50:23.500 --> 50:25.960
The treadmill will spin at the speed of light.

50:26.500 --> 50:28.860
And the dilution of our social signal will be absolute.

50:28.860 --> 50:33.500
An invisible architecture where we aren't even the residents of the house anymore.

50:34.080 --> 50:37.220
We're just the foundation slowly sinking into the quicksand.

50:37.780 --> 50:40.300
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

50:40.740 --> 50:45.760
Tomorrow, when you open your app and start scrolling your feed, take a long look at those sponsored labels.

50:46.620 --> 50:50.260
Think about the invisible auction happening in milliseconds behind the glass.

50:50.840 --> 50:54.560
And maybe look at your blocked list a little differently.

50:55.340 --> 50:56.720
Keep questioning the architecture.

50:57.480 --> 50:58.300
We'll catch you next time.

